WEBVTT

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:51.170
<v Ira Glass>Brett was standing at a subway platform, afternoon rush hour. It was crowded. And he noticed this guy-- didn't seem homeless, decent clothes-- stopping in front of each person, looking into his or her eyes, saying something, and moving on to the next person. Turns out the guy was telling people--
<v Ira Glass>布雷特当时正站在地铁站台上，正值下午下班高峰期。人很多。然后他注意到这个人——看起来不像是无家可归的人，穿着正派——在每个人面前停下来，看着他们的眼睛，说些什么，然后走向下一个人。原来这个人是在告诉大家——

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.380
<v Brett>They could stay or they had to go. They were in or they were out.
<v Brett>他们可以留下来，或者他们必须离开。他们入选了，或者被淘汰了。

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:55.600
<v Ira Glass>Literally, what would he say?
<v Ira Glass>说真的，他到底会说什么？

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:03.870
<v Brett>Well, literally, it would be, you, you're out. You're gone. You're gone. You're OK, you can stay.
<v Brett>嗯，说真的，就是，你，你被淘汰了。你走吧。你走吧。你没事，你可以留下。

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.600
<v Ira Glass>And then do people leave?
<v Ira Glass>然后人们会离开吗？

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:14.510
<v Brett>No, not at all. And no one argued with him.
<v Brett>不，完全不会。而且没有人跟他争辩。

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:19.000
<v Ira Glass>Brett wrote about the incident on his personal website, BRETTnews.
<v Ira Glass>布雷特在他的个人网站 BRETTnews 上记录了这起事件。

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:26.840
<v Ira Glass>Let me ask you to read a little bit of your account of this from your website. You write about who he decided to keep and who he decided to go.
<v Ira Glass>请允许我请你读一读你网站上对这件事的记述。你写了他是如何决定谁留下来，谁又必须离开的。

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:45.210
<v Brett>Right. These are the last few people before he reaches me. The 50-ish woman in the business suit and thick glasses is summarily dismissed. The homey in the baggy shorts and Chicago Bulls jersey makes the cut. The young immigrant mother who seems not to grasp the import of this moment is given the OK.
<v Brett>没错。这些是他找到我之前的最后几个人。那个戴着厚厚眼镜、穿着职业套装的五十岁左右的女人被当场打发走了。那个穿着宽松短裤和芝加哥公牛队球衣的哥们儿入选了。那个似乎没能领会这一刻重要性的年轻移民母亲得到了准许。

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.700
<v Ira Glass>Oh, versus you who's grasping just how important this is.
<v Ira Glass>哦，跟你相反，你可是深知这有多重要。

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:59.340
<v Brett>Right. The bookish man in the maroon cardigan sweater with balding head and red face is cut loose with particular relish.
<v Brett>没错。那个秃顶、红脸、穿着褐红色开衫毛衣的书生气男人被毫不留情地淘汰了。

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:28.180
<v Ira Glass>There is something about the judgment of strangers. When the clerk in the record store seems unimpressed by your choice of CDs. When the one cute person on the bus gives you a look like, out of my way. It's as if, by their status as strangers, they have some special instantaneous insight into who we are. Their vision isn't clouded by our feeble attempts to charm our friends and the people we work with. The guy got closer to Brett.
<v Ira Glass>陌生人的评判总是有些特别之处。当唱片店的店员似乎对你选的CD不屑一顾时。当公交车上那个可爱的人用“离我远点”的眼神看着你时。就好像凭借他们作为陌生人的身份，他们对我们是谁有着某种特殊的、即兴的洞察力。他们的视野不会被我们试图吸引朋友和同事注意的那些笨拙努力所模糊。那个人离布雷特更近了。

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.470
<v Brett>And I'm starting to feel a little nervous and aware of the fact--
<v Brett>我开始觉得有点紧张，并且意识到一个事实——

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.280
<v Ira Glass>Will I make the cut?
<v Ira Glass>我会入选吗？

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:49.580
<v Brett>It sounds so silly. We all like to think that we're evolved enough or mature enough. But when push comes to shove and a guy's going down the line rating, I found that you can't help but kind of hope that he gives you the thumbs up when your turn comes.
<v Brett>听起来太傻了。我们都喜欢认为自己足够成熟或思想足够深刻。但真到关键时刻，当一个人开始逐个打分时，我发现你还是会忍不住希望当轮到你时，他能给你竖起大拇指。

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.710
<v Ira Glass>But Brett, he's not choosing you for anything.
<v Ira Glass>但是布雷特，他又不是在选你做什么。

00:02:51.710 --> 00:03:01.230
<v Brett>No, he's not. And he didn't even look like anyone I particularly wanted to hang with, as much as one can tell from someone's appearance.
<v Brett>不，他不是。而且从一个人的外表所能看出的程度来看，他看起来甚至不像我想与之交往的人。

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.230
<v Ira Glass>You didn't really feel any need to impress this guy.
<v Ira Glass>你其实一点也不觉得需要给这个人留下好印象。

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.570
<v Brett>No, no.
<v Brett>不，不。

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:16.270
<v Ira Glass>To me, I think you're right, because this is the purest case I've ever heard of. Literally, he's picking you for nothing.
<v Ira Glass>在我看来，我觉得你说得对，因为这是我听过最纯粹的案例。说真的，他选你什么也不是。

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.120
<v Brett>Right, right.
<v Brett>对，对。

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.880
<v Ira Glass>And yet you want to be chosen.
<v Ira Glass>然而你还是想被选中。

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:27.310
<v Brett>Exactly.
<v Brett>完全正确。

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:47.880
<v Ira Glass>So the guy walks up to Brett, stands actually a little too close to him, looks in his eyes and says, you can stay. And Brett felt euphoria, a small euphoria sure. In his mind, he knew there's no reason to feel so good about this, but in his heart, it made him feel really, really happy.
<v Ira Glass>于是那家伙走向布雷特，实际上站得离他近得有点过分，看着他的眼睛说，你可以留下来。布雷特感到一阵欣喜，当然是一阵小小的欣喜。理智上，他知道根本没有理由为此感到高兴，但在内心深处，这让他感到非常非常快乐。

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.220
<v Brett>It was like, all right.
<v Brett>那种感觉就像是，太好了。

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:58.350
<v Ira Glass>You wrote in your account of this, I find myself against my own better judgment, now looking with some disdain and perhaps a tinge of pity upon those who didn't make the cut.
<v Ira Glass>你在记述中写道：“我发现自己违背了理智，现在开始带着些许轻蔑，或许还有一丝怜悯，看着那些没有入选的人。”

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:02.540
<v Brett>Sure, If you can't make this guy's cut, come on.
<v Brett>当然，如果你连这家伙的门槛都过不了，拜托。

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:09.490
<v Ira Glass>How terrible, you write, to be excluded, to be found unworthy. But no one has ever claimed life to be fair.
<v Ira Glass>你写道：“被排挤，被发现不配入选，是多么可怕的事啊。但从来没有人说过生活是公平的。”

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:16.230
<v Brett>No, they haven't.
<v Brett>不，从来没有。

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:38.960
<v Ira Glass>In a sense, this guy on the subway was committing a perfect act of kindness. The people who he gave the thumbs up to felt good. People who he told to get lost simply ignored him. No one was hurt. It was a simple act of kindness from a stranger. Which brings us to today's radio program. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it is This American Life . I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>从某种意义上说，地铁里的这个人是在行使一种完美的善举。得到他肯定的人感觉很好。被他叫走开的人只是无视了他。没有人受到伤害。这是一个陌生人带来的简单善举。这也把我们带到了今天的广播节目。来自芝加哥 WBEZ 电台和国际公共广播电台，这里是《这一美妙的生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:04:38.960 --> 00:05:27.970
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, stories of the kindness of strangers and what it leads to. And for the best perspective on the subject, all of our stories today take place in the city that has the reputation for being the unkindest city in America, New York City. Act One of our show, Tarzan Finds a Mate, in which a good deed is done with the hope of a small reward. Act Two, Runaway, in which a small good deed leads to much bigger things. Act Three, the Unkindness of Strangers, a story about a neighbor who tries to make life hell for the person next door. Act Four, Chairman of the Block, a story of 150 people who don't know each other, a tap dancer, New York cops, and Frank Sinatra. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的节目将讲述陌生人善举的故事以及它带来的影响。为了对这个主题有最好的视角，我们今天所有的故事都发生在那个以“全美最冷漠城市”闻名的城市——纽约。我们节目的第一幕，泰山寻找伴侣，讲述了一件带着微小回报期望的好事。第二幕，离家出走，讲述了一件小小的好事如何引发了更大的影响。第三幕，陌生人的冷漠，一个关于邻居试图将隔壁邻居的生活变成人间地狱的故事。第四幕，街区主席，一个关于150个互不相识的人、一个踢踏舞演员、纽约警察和弗兰克·辛纳屈的故事。请继续收听。

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:39.300
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Tarzan Finds a Mate. This true story of a good deed someone tries to do for a stranger comes from Joel Kostman who is a locksmith in New York.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，泰山寻找伴侣。这个关于某人试图为陌生人做好事的真实故事来自乔尔·科斯特曼，他是纽约的一名锁匠。

00:05:39.300 --> 00:06:03.200
<v Joel Kostman>It's a little past midnight and I'd just returned home from dropping my girlfriend Deborah off at the airport. Late at night is the only time of day I like the way my block looks. There are no panhandlers. The parking lots are all empty. And the constant noise you hear in the daytime from the exiting Lincoln Tunnel traffic is minimized. It almost looks like a real street, a place where people live.
<v Joel Kostman>时间刚过午夜，我刚从机场送女朋友黛博拉回来。深夜是一天中我唯一喜欢自己街区样子的时间。没有乞丐。停车场全空着。白天林肯隧道出口交通产生的持续噪音也被降到了最低。它看起来几乎像一条真正的街道，一个人们生活的地方。

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:25.440
<v Joel Kostman>Remarkably, I find a parking space right in front of my building. I sit in the car with the motor running listening to the radio and thinking about Deborah. We live together. This morning, I thought we were in love. Tonight, I'm not sure if I'm ever going to see her again. The DJ plays a Freddie and the Dreamers tune, "I'm Telling You Now."
<v Joel Kostman>奇迹般地，我在大楼正前方找到了一个停车位。我坐在车里，发动机开着，听着收音机，想着黛博拉。我们住在一起。今天早上，我还以为我们深陷爱河。今晚，我却不确定以后是否还能见到她。DJ播放了一首“弗雷迪和梦想者”乐队的曲子，《我现在就告诉你》。

00:06:25.440 --> 00:07:04.260
<v Joel Kostman>Suddenly I hear someone across the street yell something. I look up and a young woman is standing next to a red sports car, her head resting on the roof. "Damn, damn, damn," she moans, pounding an alternate fist down with each word. She steps back, her hands on her hips, and looks around as if for a lost child. She has straight blond hair which hangs down to her shoulders. She's wearing tight blue jeans, a yellow shirt unbuttoned down to her cleavage, and black spike heels. She's got on bright red lipstick and gold interlocking circles for earrings. They jangle when she turns her head.
<v Joel Kostman>突然我听到街对面有人大喊了一声。我抬头看去，一个年轻女子正站在一辆红色跑车旁，头靠在车顶上。“该死，该死，该死，”她呻吟着，每说一个词就轮流砸一下拳头。她退后一步，双手叉腰，环顾四周，仿佛在找一个走失的孩子。她留着直直的金发，垂到肩头。她穿着紧身牛仔裤，黄色衬衫解开扣子露出乳沟，脚踩黑色细高跟鞋。她涂着亮红色的口红，戴着金色的互锁圆环耳环。当她转头时，耳环叮当作响。

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:18.540
<v Joel Kostman>"Oh damn," she says again, and throws her bag at the car. It's a Porsche. I shut off my engine and get out. I don't want to scare her so I call from across the street.
<v Joel Kostman>“哦，该死，”她又说了一遍，把包扔向车子。那是一辆保时捷。我关掉发动机下了车。我不想吓到她，所以我在街对面喊道。

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:31.860
<v Joel Kostman>"Excuse me, you need some help?" She's bending down on the sidewalk, picking up some things that fell out of her bag. She looks up and for a second I think she's going to scream. Then she smiles.
<v Joel Kostman>“打扰一下，你需要帮忙吗？”她正弯腰在人行道上捡从包里掉出来的一些东西。她抬起头，有一秒钟我以为她要尖叫。然后她笑了。

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:48.890
<v Joel Kostman>"I locked my [BLEEP] keys in the car," she says as she stands up. "I can't believe I did this." Her hands do a kind of Betty Boop thing. I decide that she's Jersey, here for a concert at the Garden. She just has that Jersey feel.
<v Joel Kostman>“我把[脏话]钥匙锁在车里了，”她站起身来说道。“真不敢相信我干了这种事。”她的双手做出一种贝蒂·布普式的动作。我断定她是新泽西人，来这里是为了看花园广场的音乐会。她身上有一种新泽西的气质。

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:52.870
<v Joel Kostman>"You're in luck," I say, still from across the street.
<v Joel Kostman>“你真走运，”我说道，依然站在街对面。

00:07:52.870 --> 00:08:17.860
<v Joel Kostman>She purses her lips and nods. "Why, you going to take me out for a drink until the tow truck gets here?" She laughs but starts coughing in the middle. I go to my trunk and remove my car lockout stuff. A pretty, stranded Jersey girl with a sense of humor no less, I say to myself. There's something in her face that reminds me of a young Jessica Lange.
<v Joel Kostman>她撅起嘴唇点点头。“怎么，你要带我去喝一杯，直到拖车来吗？”她笑了，但笑到一半咳嗽起来。我走到后备箱拿我的开锁工具。一个漂亮、滞留街头的新泽西女孩，而且还挺有幽默感，我对自己说。她脸上的某些特征让我想起了年轻时的杰西卡·兰格。

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:52.580
<v Joel Kostman>I cross the street with my slim jim in one hand. It's a thin, silvery piece of metal about two feet long with some notches cut out at the bottom used to open car doors. I carry it at my side like a sword, like a knight would. In my other hand I grasp my tool kit. In my shirt pocket is the little leather case that contains my picks, which I bring just in case I run into any trouble. I step up on the sidewalk next to her. "I'm a locksmith," I announce. I love these moments when I get to play the hero.
<v Joel Kostman>我手里拿着一根细铁条（slim jim）过了马路。这是一根大约两英尺长的纤细银色金属片，底部有一些切口，用来打开车门。我把它像宝剑一样拿在身侧，就像骑士那样。另一只手我抓着工具箱。衬衫口袋里装着一个小皮套，里面放着我的拨锁工具，我带着它是以防遇到什么麻烦。我走到她身旁的人行道上。“我是个锁匠，”我宣布。我喜欢这种扮演英雄的时刻。

00:08:52.580 --> 00:09:33.330
<v Joel Kostman>She has a loopy smile on her face which stays there even as her expression slowly changes. I can smell the alcohol on her breath. She looks at the slim jim and then back at my face. "No [BLEEP]," she says. "Well, I guess it's my lucky day." She lays a hand on my shoulder like we're old pals. She squeezes and then leans on me a little. Her head floats around in front of my face. "You open it up and the drinks are on me," she says in a kind of half growl.
<v Joel Kostman>她脸上带着一种迷糊的微笑，即使她的表情慢慢改变，那笑容也依然挂在脸上。我能闻到她呼吸中的酒精味。她看了看细铁条，又看了看我的脸。“真不是[脏话]，”她说。“好吧，我想我今天走运了。”她把一只手搭在我的肩膀上，就像我们是老朋友一样。她捏了捏，然后稍微靠在我身上。她的头在我脸前晃来晃去。“你把它打开，酒我请，”她用一种半低吼的语气说。

00:09:33.330 --> 00:10:04.000
<v Joel Kostman>I peer into the car window and see the keys dangling from the ignition. There are a couple of empty beer bottles on the floor on the passenger side. I look back at the woman. She's got a cigarette going now. At that moment, from behind us, we hear a long, clear Tarzan call. It's a perfect imitation, lasting about 10 seconds, complete with the jungle yodels in the middle. "What the hell was that," the woman asks.
<v Joel Kostman>我透过车窗往里看，看到钥匙挂在点火开关上。副驾驶座位的地板上有几个空啤酒瓶。我回头看着那个女人。她现在点上了一支烟。就在这时，从我们身后，我们听到一声悠长、清脆的泰山叫声。这是一个完美的模仿，持续了大约10秒钟，中间还夹杂着丛林的约德尔唱法。“刚才那是什么鬼东西，”那女人问。

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:17.730
<v Joel Kostman>She steps out toward the street and leans her head way back. She looks up at the parking structure that's a block north on 31st Street. I get a real good look at her then. "That's Tarzan," I say.
<v Joel Kostman>她向街面上迈出一步，把头往后仰。她抬头看着31st街北边一个街区的停车场。这时我把她看得很真切。“那是泰山，”我说。

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:27.420
<v Joel Kostman>She tilts her head to the side, half closes her right eye, and raises her left eyebrow. "Friend of yours?" she asks.
<v Joel Kostman>她把头偏向一边，半闭着右眼，挑起左眉。“你的朋友？”她问。

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:30.840
<v Joel Kostman>"I think he works in the parking structure," I say.
<v Joel Kostman>“我想他在停车场工作，”我说。

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:58.700
<v Joel Kostman>"Oh," she says, with a look on her face that says that explains everything. She puts her hands behind her and leans back. I momentarily think about Deborah. The woman in front of me couldn't be more different in appearance. She's as tall as I am with an accent out of a Stallone movie. She looks like a wild, fun-loving gal, good working-class stock. I wonder what she's like when she's sober.
<v Joel Kostman>“哦，”她说，脸上露出一副“这就说得通了”的表情。她把双手背在身后，身体向后靠。我脑子里一闪而过黛博拉。眼前的这个女人在外表上简直不能再不同了。她和我一样高，带着史泰龙电影里的口音。她看起来像个狂野、热爱聚会的姑娘，根正苗红的工人阶级。我想知道她清醒时是什么样子。

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:19.850
<v Joel Kostman>"So you going to do your thing, or what?" the woman asks. I hold up my slim jim. "Action," she says. I dip my slim jim into the car door, feeling around. I try different angles, different depths. Nothing happens. she hops off the hood of the car and stands next to me. "No luck?" she asks.
<v Joel Kostman>“所以你要大显身手了，还是怎样？”那女人问。我举起我的细铁条。“开始吧，”她说。我把细铁条插进车门缝隙里，摸索着。我尝试不同的角度，不同的深度。什么也没发生。她从车前盖上跳下来，站在我旁边。“没戏？”她问。

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:22.110
<v Joel Kostman>"Not yet."
<v Joel Kostman>“还没有。”

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:42.070
<v Joel Kostman>It's a hot night. She takes a tissue from her bag and says, "here, you're sweating buckets." I wipe my forehead. The tissue smells like perfume. She removes another one and dabs at her neck and chest. She flaps her hand in front of her face like a fan. "I've got air conditioning in there once you get it open," she says.
<v Joel Kostman>这是一个炎热的夜晚。她从包里拿出一张纸巾说：“给你，你满头大汗。”我擦了擦额头。纸巾闻起来有香水的味道。她又拿出一张，擦了擦脖子和胸口。她在脸前像扇子一样扇着手。“你一旦把它打开，里面就有空调了，”她说。

00:11:42.070 --> 00:12:11.210
<v Joel Kostman>"I'll have it open in a minute," I say. I start thinking about her behind the wheel of the car and where we'll go. She rummages around in the bag again and produces a pack of cigarettes. She lights one up, takes a drag, and blows the smoke up toward the sky. I haven't smoked in 10 years, but it still resonates for me, how it feels, how sexy it looks, which is why I think people do it. She offers me one. "No thanks," I say.
<v Joel Kostman>“我一会儿就能打开，”我说。我开始想象她坐在方向盘后面的样子以及我们要去哪里。她在包里又翻找起来，掏出一包烟。她点燃一支，吸了一口，把烟吐向天空。我已经10年没抽烟了，但它对我依然很有共鸣，那种感觉，那种看起来性感的姿态，这就是为什么我认为人们会抽烟。她递给我一支。“不用了谢谢，”我说。

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:23.390
<v Joel Kostman>"Sorry I don't have anything stronger." She smiles. I smile back.
<v Joel Kostman>“抱歉我没有更烈的酒。”她笑了。我也回了个笑。

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:40.700
<v Joel Kostman>She strikes a pose that smokers do, right arm bent at the elbow, forearm across the body. The left elbow rests on the right wrist and the forearm goes straight up, the fingers at the lips. I pull the slim jim out. "Harder than you thought, huh?" She says.
<v Joel Kostman>她摆出了一个烟民常有的姿势，右肘弯曲，前臂横在胸前。左肘搭在右腕上，前臂笔直向上，手指贴着嘴唇。我把细铁条抽了出来。“比想象的要难，对吧？”她说。

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:43.440
<v Joel Kostman>"Some foreign cars are tough," I say.
<v Joel Kostman>“有些外国车挺难搞的，”我说。

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:45.130
<v Joel Kostman>"Can I try?"
<v Joel Kostman>“我能试试吗？”

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:46.750
<v Joel Kostman>"You want to try?"
<v Joel Kostman>“你想试试？”

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:52.330
<v Joel Kostman>"Yeah. Who knows? Maybe it'll be like beginners luck. It looks like fun."
<v Joel Kostman>“对啊。谁知道呢？也许会有初学者的运气呢。看起来挺好玩的。”

00:12:52.330 --> 00:13:00.630
<v Joel Kostman>I don't know why, but I say, "sure." I slide the slim jim through the space between the window and the rubber stripping. She takes the end of it.
<v Joel Kostman>我不知道为什么，但我说：“好吧。”我把细铁条滑进车窗和橡胶嵌条之间的空隙里。她接住了它的末端。

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:05.630
<v Joel Kostman>"Like this?" she asks, moving it back and forth like a slot machine handle.
<v Joel Kostman>“像这样吗？”她问，像拉老虎机手柄一样前后移动着它。

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:28.780
<v Joel Kostman>"No," I say. "Actually, you kind of go like this." I take her hand, move it up and down slowly, bobbing the end of the tool slightly from side to side. We're doing a kind of slim jim tango, dipping in and out and up and down. The car door won't open, but she doesn't even seem perturbed. It's like we're playing a game. "I'm going to try to pick it," I say.
<v Joel Kostman>“不对，”我说。“实际上，你应该这样做。”我抓住她的手，上下缓缓移动，让工具的末端稍微左右晃动。我们在大跳一种细铁条探戈，时而深入时而抽出，时而上下起伏。车门还是打不开，但她看起来一点也不慌乱。就像我们在玩游戏一样。“我要试着用单钩开锁，”我说。

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:30.960
<v Joel Kostman>"Can I still do this?" she asks.
<v Joel Kostman>“我还能这么做吗？”她问。

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:47.320
<v Joel Kostman>"Sure. I'll work on the other door." As I step away, we suddenly hear Tarzan again. It's louder this time. He must be on a lower floor. It's a particularly beautiful call, and he really trails out the last note.
<v Joel Kostman>“当然。我去搞定另一扇门。”当我走开时，我们突然又听到了泰山的叫声。这次声音更大了。他一定在更低的楼层。这是一声特别美妙的叫声，他把最后一个音拖得老长。

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:57.140
<v Joel Kostman>The woman bends over double and slaps her thighs in rapid fashion. "I love that," she cries. "That is just fabulous."
<v Joel Kostman>那女人弯下腰，飞快地拍打着大腿。“我太喜欢这个了，”她大喊。“这简直太棒了。”

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:04.040
<v Joel Kostman>"Local color," I say. I take my can of WD-40 and lubricate the cylinder.
<v Joel Kostman>“地方特色，”我说。我拿起我的WD-40润滑剂喷了喷锁芯。

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:17.200
<v Joel Kostman>"That guy should be on TV, or something." I squat by the other side of the car. I insert the tension bar in the cylinder and hold it down with my thumb. And then I work the rake through several times.
<v Joel Kostman>“那家伙应该上电视，或者干点别的。”我蹲在车的另一侧。我把张力杆插入锁芯，用大拇指按住。然后我把拨锁器在里面来回耙了几次。

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:52.830
<v Joel Kostman>After a couple of minutes, I'm starting to get frustrated. I say, "I'll be right back. I'm going to get something else." I run to my car and remove the metal doweling I bought on Canal Street for this very situation. It's long and sturdy but pliable. I bring it back across the street. "Here," I say, "you hold this." She lays the slim jim down on the sidewalk. I insert a large screwdriver between the door and the body of the car. "Just put a little pressure on it. Like this," I say as I push back. "That'll give me room to maneuver."
<v Joel Kostman>几分钟后，我开始感到沮丧。我说：“我马上回来。我去拿点别的东西。”我跑向我的车，拿出了我为了这种特殊情况在运河街买的金属杆。它又长又结实，但具有延展性。我把它拿过街来。“给你，”我说，“你拿着这个。”她把细铁条放在人行道上。我在车门和车身之间插入一把大号螺丝刀。“稍微加点力。就这样，”我一边往回推一边说。“这能给我留出操作空间。”

00:14:52.830 --> 00:15:19.930
<v Joel Kostman>She stands behind me and pushes back on the screwdriver. I bend the end of the dowel into an L and slide it in. I push it toward the button which is in an impossible spot on the door panel, just behind the handle. I'm thinking, this car really is a pain in the ass. I wiggle my end of the dowel and poke at the area of the button, but I keep missing. "How does that button work?" I ask. "Do you push it forward, or in, or what?"
<v Joel Kostman>她站在我身后，往回推着螺丝刀。我把金属杆的末端弯成L形并滑了进去。我把它推向门板上一个极其刁钻的位置，就在把手后面。我想着，这车可真够让人头疼的。我晃动着金属杆的末端，去戳按钮的区域，但总是戳不到。“那个按钮是怎么操作的？”我问，“你是往前推，还是往里按，还是怎样？”

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:38.210
<v Joel Kostman>"Gee," she says. "I don't know. Let me think." She bobs her head slowly side to side and finally says, "in, I think. I think." I keep poking at the button. Once I hit it square on and let out a whoop. But when I try the door it doesn't open.
<v Joel Kostman>“天哪，”她说。“我不知道。让我想想。”她慢慢地左右晃着头，最后说：“往里按，我想。我想是的。”我继续戳着那个按钮。有一次我正正地击中了它，发出了一声欢呼。但当我试着开车门时，它还是打不开。

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:42.800
<v Joel Kostman>"Damn," I yell and slam my fist down on top of the Porsche.
<v Joel Kostman>“该死，”我大喊着，一拳砸在保时捷的车顶上。

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:55.670
<v Joel Kostman>"Hey," she says, "come on. We'll get it." She puts her hand on my arm. "You know, you're really a sweet guy for helping me out." She leans forward and kisses me on the cheek.
<v Joel Kostman>“嘿，”她说，“别这样。我们能打开的。”她把手放在我的胳膊上。“你知道吗，你帮了我大忙，真的是个很温柔的家伙。”她身体前倾，吻了吻我的脸颊。

00:15:55.670 --> 00:16:09.040
<v Joel Kostman>When Deborah said goodbye to me at the airport, she said, "maybe you and I should take the next few days to reevaluate." Then she put her hand on my upper arm and kissed me on the cheek.
<v Joel Kostman>黛博拉在机场跟我道别时说：“也许你我都该花几天时间重新评估一下。”接着她把手搭在我的大臂上，吻了吻我的脸颊。

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:16.410
<v Joel Kostman>"Let's try it again," the woman says. "Listen," I say, "I'm sorry. I'm just frustrated. I usually don't have this much trouble."
<v Joel Kostman>“我们再试一次，”那女人说。“听着，”我说，“我很抱歉。我只是很沮丧。我平时没这么费劲的。”

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:23.740
<v Joel Kostman>"It's OK," she says. "I know we're going to get it this time." She punches the air like a cheerleader.
<v Joel Kostman>“没关系，”她说。“我知道这次我们一定能行。”她像拉拉队员一样兴奋地挥了挥拳头。

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:55.750
<v Joel Kostman>We try again. I twist the dowel around to get it in just the right position and then push it forward with my hand so it will come smashing into the button. The door doesn't open. I do it again and again. As my body bumps into the woman's and rubs up against her, I get more and more crazy. I can feel my hero status evaporating. Finally, after about 15 minutes, she says, "you know, I think if I push this way with the screwdriver, it'll make more room. I think it'll be a lot easier for you."
<v Joel Kostman>我们再试一次。我把木钉转动到一个正好的位置，然后用手向前推，好让它猛烈地撞击到按钮上。门没有打开。我一遍又一遍地试。随着我的身体撞上那个女人并与她擦蹭，我变得越来越抓狂。我能感觉到我英雄的地位正在蒸发。最后，大约15分钟后，她说道：“你知道的，我觉得如果我用螺丝刀这样推一下，就能腾出更多空间。我觉得对你来说会容易得多。”

00:16:55.750 --> 00:17:30.280
<v Joel Kostman>Before I can stop her, she leans against the car and pushes. There's a loud crack. The window shatters into pieces which fall on the sidewalk at our feet. I look at her face. Her mouth is wide open, her shoulders raised in embarrassment. Then, suddenly, she opens the door, brushes the glass off the seat with her bag and gets in. "Well, I got to go," she says. She starts the car. "I don't know how to thank you." She speeds off toward Eighth Avenue. "Goodbye," she calls out.
<v Joel Kostman>没等我阻止她，她就靠在车上推了起来。只听“啪”的一声巨响。车窗碎成了几块，落在了我们脚边的行人道上。我看着她的脸。她嘴巴张得大大的，双肩因尴尬而耸起。接着，她突然打开车门，用包扫掉座位上的玻璃碎渣，然后坐了进去。“好吧，我得走了，”她说。她发动了汽车。“我都不知道该怎么感谢你。”她向第八大道方向飞驰而去。“再见，”她喊道。

00:17:30.280 --> 00:18:07.260
<v Joel Kostman>I am stunned by the swiftness of her departure. As I watch her drive off, her hand waving out the window, Tarzan gives his grand finale. His voice is so strong that it sounds like he's right behind me. His call begins with one beautiful, long, sustained note. He holds it longer than I have ever heard before. Then he leaps into a spectacular trill which ends with another gorgeous full note and follows this with a second trill, which trails off into a final, eerie, haunting tone.
<v Joel Kostman>我被她离去的速度惊呆了。看着她开车离去，她的手在窗外挥舞着，泰山献上了他的压轴大戏。他的声音是如此洪亮，听起来就像他就在我身后。他的呼喊始于一个优美、长久、持续的音符。他这个音维持的时间比我以往听过的任何人都要长。接着，他纵身跃入一段精彩的颤音，以另一个华丽的全音结束，紧接着是第二段颤音，最后渐渐消散为一个终结的、诡异而萦绕心头的音调。

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:33.740
<v Joel Kostman>I turn to face the parking structure. I'm standing in the middle of a pile of my discarded tools and broken glass. I lean my head way back, looking up at the sky. I cup my hands around my mouth, take the deepest possible breath, and yell at the top of my lungs, "shut the hell up!"
<v Joel Kostman>我转过身面对停车场建筑。我正站在一堆被我扔掉的工具和碎玻璃的中间。我把头向后仰，望向天空。我用双手围住嘴巴，深深地吸了一口气，然后用尽全身力气大喊：“给老子闭嘴！”

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:41.330
<v Ira Glass>Joel Kostman's stories of his life as a locksmith are in his book Keys to the City.
<v Ira Glass>乔尔·科斯特曼（Joel Kostman）讲述他作为开锁匠生活的的故事收录在他的书《城市之钥》（Keys to the City）中。

00:18:41.330 --> 00:19:03.340
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "BOLT" BY BEN LEE]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 本·李的《BOLT》]

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:23.970
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Runaway. When you commit an act of kindness for a stranger, where can it lead? In 1940, Jack Geiger was 14 years old, not getting along with his parents. Because of the odd rules of the New York City schools at that time, he had actually finished high school, but no college would let him in so young. He wasn't getting along with his parents, fought with them all the time, and then he went to see a play.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，出走。当你对一个陌生人行善时，这会走向何方？1940年，杰克·盖格（Jack Geiger）14岁，和父母关系不好。由于当时纽约市学校的一些奇葩规定，他其实已经读完了高中，但没有哪所大学愿意录取这么年轻的他。他和父母相处得不好，总是和他们吵架，然后他去看了一场戏剧。

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:53.810
<v Jack Geiger>Native Son, Orson Welles' Mercury Theatre Production of the Richard Wright novel which starred a black actor named Canada Lee. And I was very moved by that. And with the brashness of a 14-year-old, I went backstage afterwards and found Canada Lee and hung around and talked with him a while. And I liked that so much that I did that three or four more times.
<v Jack Geiger>《原生之子》（Native Son），奥逊·威尔斯的水星剧院制作的理查德·赖特的小说，由一位名叫卡纳达·李（Canada Lee）的黑人演员主演。我被深深地打动了。带着14岁少年的鲁莽，散场后我去了后台，找到了卡纳达·李，在那里闲逛并和他聊了一会儿。我太喜欢这种感觉了，于是我又这么做了三四次。

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:57.460
<v Ira Glass>Do you recall what it is that you were talking to him about, what you wanted to him about?
<v Ira Glass>你还记得你们当时在谈论什么吗，你想和他谈些什么？

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:37.610
<v Jack Geiger>Well, we started out talking about the play and Richard Wright and the main character, Bigger Thomas, and race relations in the United States. And pretty soon we got around to-- by the second or third conversation at least-- what was going on in my life and what I wanted to do and my conflicts and so on. He learned a lot more about me than I did about him, I think, at that point in those conversations.
<v Jack Geiger>嗯，我们一开始是谈论那部戏剧、理查德·赖特以及主角比格·托马斯（Bigger Thomas），还有美国的种族关系。很快我们就聊到了——至少到了第二或第三次谈话时——我生活中的现状、我想做什么以及我的冲突等等。我想在当时的那些谈话中，他对我了解得比我对他的了解要多得多。

00:20:37.610 --> 00:21:31.640
<v Jack Geiger>And then one day when the conflict at home just a lot tougher, I waited until a Sunday when I knew there was no performance of Native Son . And my folks were out, and I packed a bag, and I took a subway up to the top of Sugar Hill in Harlem, 555 Edgecombe Avenue, where I knew that Canada had a penthouse. And I went up and rang the doorbell. And he was home and opened the door. And I said, Lee, the stuff at home is just getting too much and I thought maybe I could stay here for a while. Cold, just like that. And he kind of looked around and pointed to a couch in the living room and said, well, I guess you could sleep over there.
<v Jack Geiger>然后有一天，当家里的矛盾变得更加激烈时，我等到了一个星期天，我知道那天没有《原生之子》的演出。我家人都出去了，我收拾好行李，坐地铁去了哈林区的糖山（Sugar Hill）顶端，即埃奇科姆大道555号，我知道卡纳达在那里有一套顶层公寓。我走上去按了门铃。他在家，并打开了门。我说，李，家里的情况实在让人难以忍受，我想我或许可以在这里呆上一段时间。冷冰冰的，就这么直接说了。他环顾了一下四周，指着客厅的一张沙发说，好吧，我想你可以在那儿睡。

00:21:31.640 --> 00:22:08.690
<v Jack Geiger>After I had gone to sleep that evening, I later learned, he called my folks and said, look, I'll send him back in the morning. But why don't you let him stay here, because I'm not sure where he's going to land the next time. And my parents must have been so exhausted by all of this that they agreed, at least tentatively. And that was the beginning of a whole year that I really lived there and had one of the great educational experiences of my life.
<v Jack Geiger>那天晚上我睡着后，我后来了解到，他给我的家人打电话说，听着，我会在早上把他送回去。但你们为什么不让他留在这儿呢，因为我不敢确定他下次会落脚在哪里。我的父母一定是被这一切折磨得筋疲力尽了，所以他们同意了，至少是暂时同意了。那开启了整整一年的时间，我真的就住在那里，并拥有了我一生中最伟大的教育经历之一。

00:22:08.690 --> 00:23:06.070
<v Jack Geiger>Through that apartment, over that year that I remember, came the kind of the cream of the Harlem theatrical, sporting, civil rights, political, and intellectual world. And I had the chance to sit around evening after evening, many weekends, listening to Langston Hughes, William Saroyan, Adam Clayton Powell, Billy Strayhorn, Duke Ellington's arranger, Richard Wright, who came back once from exile and stopped in. And what I remember most is listening to people, listening to the conversations about World War II and race and democracy, segregation in the armed forces, what was happening in the South, what was happening in New York City.
<v Jack Geiger>据我回忆，在那一年里，通过那套公寓来来往往的都是哈林区戏剧、体育、民权、政治和知识界的最顶尖人物。我有很多机会在许多周末的傍晚围坐在一起，聆听兰斯顿·休斯（Langston Hughes）、威廉·萨罗扬（William Saroyan）、亚当·克莱顿·鲍威尔（Adam Clayton Powell）、比利·斯特霍恩（Billy Strayhorn，艾灵顿公爵的编曲）、从流亡中回来并顺道拜访的理查德·赖特。我记忆最深的是倾听人们说话，聆听关于第二次世界大战、种族与民主、军队中的种族隔离、南方的局势以及纽约市发生的事情的对话。

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:17.040
<v Ira Glass>Let me ask you to assess what you think your parents' reaction was when they got this first call from Canada Lee, to have their white, Jewish, middle-class son suddenly up living with a black man in Harlem in the early '40s.
<v Ira Glass>请允许我问一下，你认为你的父母在接到卡纳达·李打来的第一个电话时会有什么反应？他们这个白人、犹太裔、中产阶级儿子突然在40年代初跑去哈林区跟一个黑人同住。

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:58.120
<v Jack Geiger>Well, I think they were exhausted. We had had so much struggle. A little later, I remember, further on, when we were talking to each other again, Lee was giving a party and invited my parents, who, with great trepidation, came up to Harlem at night-- I don't think they had ever done that before-- and came to this party. And Canada, I remember, turned to my mother and said, hey, I'm a bachelor. Do you think you could help us out in the kitchen? It was a big party. And my mother said, sure.
<v Jack Geiger>嗯，我想他们已经筋疲力尽了。我们经历了太多的挣扎。后来我记得，再往后，当我们重新开始交谈时，李要办一场派对，并邀请了我的父母。他们带着巨大的忐忑，在夜里来到了哈林区——我想他们以前从来没这么做过——并参加了这个派对。我记得卡纳达转身对我的母亲说：“嘿，我是个单身汉。你觉得你能来厨房帮帮忙吗？”那是一场很大的派对。我母亲说：“当然可以。”

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:27.120
<v Jack Geiger>The next day, I talked to my mother on the phone and she said she had had the most wonderful time, had spent a couple of hours in the kitchen with this wonderful man, and they'd had all this conversation. And I said, who was it? She said, well, she didn't know. She'd never gotten the name. And I said, well, describe him. And she discovered that she had spent two hours chatting with Langston Hughes and was mortified that she had never realized it.
<v Jack Geiger>第二天，我在电话里和我母亲交谈，她说她度过了一段最美妙的时光，在这个美妙的男人身边在厨房里度过了几个小时，他们聊了那么多。我说：“那是谁？”她说，嗯，她不知道。她从来没有问过名字。我说，好吧，描述一下他。然后她发现自己竟然和兰斯顿·休斯聊了两个小时的天，而她此前从未意识到这一点，这让她感到十分窘迫。

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:32.350
<v Ira Glass>What did they talk about?
<v Ira Glass>他们聊了些什么？

00:24:32.350 --> 00:25:00.690
<v Jack Geiger>You had to have met Langston Hughes to know. He was as comfortable as an old shoe. And I'm sure they talked about cooking. And I'm sure they talked about whatever else my mother wanted to talk about. And she never quite got over it and still recalled it.
<v Jack Geiger>你必须见过兰斯顿·休斯才知道。他随和得就像一双穿旧的鞋子。我敢说他们聊了烹饪。我敢说他们还聊了任何我母亲想聊的其他事情。她对此一直念念不忘，至今仍记忆犹新。

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:20.460
<v Jack Geiger>During that year, he was kind of an informal, surrogate father. And I was in that stage where I wasn't going to take anything from the parents I was fighting with. So he staked me to a good bit of my first year at college when I found a place that would finally let me in. And--
<v Jack Geiger>在那一年里，他有点像一个非正式的代理父亲。而当时我正处于那种绝不接受和我争吵的父母施舍的阶段。所以当我找到一所最终愿意录取我的大学时，他资助了我大一的很大一部分学费。然后——

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:21.920
<v Ira Glass>So he paid for your school?
<v Ira Glass>所以他为你付了学费？

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:23.890
<v Jack Geiger>Well, he loaned me the money.
<v Jack Geiger>嗯，他借钱给我。

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:25.620
<v Ira Glass>Instead of your parents?
<v Ira Glass>而不是你的父母？

00:25:25.620 --> 00:26:10.620
<v Jack Geiger>Yeah. It wasn't until a little later that I figured out why, unconsciously maybe, I had made the choice that I did. It turned out, although I didn't know it at the time, that Canada Lee himself had grown up in a pretty strict, middle-class West Indian family. And he had, he told me, the same kind of dissatisfactions and mixed-up feelings that I'd had about his relationship with his family, what he wanted to do. And he ran away. And I think that experience may have had something to do with his kindness in taking this strange kid in and making a sort of second home for him.
<v Jack Geiger>是的。直到后来我才想明白，为什么我当时会做出那样的选择，也许是潜意识里的。事实证明，虽然我当时并不知道，但卡纳达·李本人就是在西印度群岛的一个相当严格的中产阶级家庭中长大的。他告诉我，他曾有过和我一样的对家庭关系的不满和矛盾心情，以及对自己想做什么的迷茫。然后他出走了。我想那段经历可能在某种程度上促成了他的善良，收留了这个陌生的孩子，并为他建立了一个某种意义上的第二个家。

00:26:36.690 --> 00:27:00.920
<v Jack Geiger>The thing I've thought about-- a lot, without ever really finding an answer-- is what kind of clues did I have that said hey, this is a guy that I can approach in this way, a scrawny kid with a suitcase on a Sunday night, and have some kind of shot at getting taken in? I was either very insightful or very lucky. And I think it was mostly luck.
<v Jack Geiger>我一直在思考的一件事——思考了很久，却从未真正找到答案——那就是，我当时究竟从哪里得到了一些线索，让我觉得：嘿，这是一个我可以这样去接近的人，一个在周日晚上带着手提箱瘦骨嶙峋的孩子，并且有希望被收留？我要么是极具洞察力，要么就是极其幸运。而我认为大部分还是运气。

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:04.330
<v Ira Glass>Do you think there were clues that you were given though?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得你当时确实得到过什么线索吗？

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:31.860
<v Jack Geiger>I think there must've clues, just in the fact that here was a Broadway star who was hanging around backstage talking with a kid about life and about his troubles. That's a signal that I don't think anybody could have missed.
<v Jack Geiger>我想一定是有线索的，就凭这是一个百老汇明星，居然会在后台闲逛，和一个孩子聊生活聊他的烦恼。这是一个我认为任何人都不会错过的信号。

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:57.420
<v Ira Glass>Jack lived with Canada Lee for a year. Sometimes Lee's teenage son would be there too. Jack went to college, enlisted in the Merchant Marines during World War II, serving on the only ship with a black captain and integrated crew of officers, the SS Booker T. Washington. When Jack would could come to New York, on school break or from the Merchant Marines, he would stay with Canada Lee. Then, on one of Jack's trips home, Canada Lee told Jack that he was pressed for cash, asked if he could borrow $1,000.
<v Ira Glass>杰克和卡纳达·李一起生活了一年。有时李十几岁的儿子也会在那里。杰克上了大学，在第二次世界大战期间加入了商船队，在唯一一艘拥有黑人船长和多元族裔军官船员的船——“布克·T·华盛顿号”（SS Booker T. Washington）上服役。每当杰克放假或从商船队休假回到纽约时，他都会住在卡纳达·李那里。后来，在杰克的一次回家之旅中，卡纳达·李告诉杰克他手头有点紧，问能不能借1000美元。

00:27:57.420 --> 00:28:26.140
<v Jack Geiger>And I said, sure. And I loaned it to him, and I came back from the next trip, and he paid me back. And it took me a while, in retrospect, to figure out that he didn't need the $1,000. He was just changing the nature of the relationship between us and saying, hey, now you're grown up. Now you're an adult. And I'm not your dad anymore. We're partners. I can borrow money from you just the way you borrowed money from me.
<v Jack Geiger>我说，当然可以。我借给了他，当我下次航行回来时，他把钱还给了我。事后我花了好一会儿才明白，他根本不需要那1000美元。他只是在改变我们之间关系的性质，并且在说：嘿，你现在长大了。你现在是个成年人了。我不再是你的老爸了。我们是伙伴。我可以像你以前向我借钱那样向你借钱。

00:28:26.140 --> 00:28:28.510
<v Ira Glass>As a way of evening the scales.
<v Ira Glass>以此来拉平天平。

00:28:28.510 --> 00:28:41.850
<v Jack Geiger>Yeah.
<v Jack Geiger>对。

00:28:41.850 --> 00:29:11.490
<v Ira Glass>As he got older, Jack became a journalist, then a doctor, active in the Civil Rights Movement, went to Mississippi with the civil rights workers in the early '60s. Was a founder of Physicians for Social Responsibility and later Physicians for Human Rights, started community health centers in Mississippi and in South Africa. In this country, those health centers eventually led to 900 community health centers. They now provide primary care for more than 14 million low-income people across the country. Jack Geiger says he'd never had moved so deeply into these worlds so quickly if not for his experience with Canada Lee.
<v Ira Glass>随着年龄的增长，杰克先成为了一名记者，然后成为了一名医生，积极投身于民权运动，并在60年代初与民权工作者一起去了密西西比州。他是“社会责任医师协会”（Physicians for Social Responsibility）的创始人之一，后来又创立了“人权医师协会”（Physicians for Human Rights），在密西西比州和南非创办了社区卫生中心。在这个国家，这些卫生中心最终发展成为了900个社区卫生中心。它们现在为全国1400多万低收入人群提供初级医疗服务。杰克·格格说，要不是因为卡纳达·李的经历，他绝不可能如此迅速、如此深入地涉足这些领域。

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:36.540
<v Jack Geiger>It's a relationship, very obviously, that has stayed with me ever since. Most of my life and work has, in one way or another, involved civil rights and human rights. It must be one of the reasons why I became a physician, of wanting to look out for people who were in trouble.
<v Jack Geiger>很显然，这段关系一直伴随着我直到今天。我大部分的生活和工作，以这样或那样的方式，都与民权和人权有关。这一定是我成为一名医生的原因之一，因为我想照顾那些处于困境中的人。

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:50.370
<v Ira Glass>Was it your impression that other people had extended this kind of act of kindness to him that he then extended to you? Or that he had yearned that someone would have taken him in the way that he took you in?
<v Ira Glass>你的印象是，其他人曾对他施以这种善举，然后他才将这种善举延伸到了你身上？还是说他一直渴望有人能像他接纳你那样接纳他？

00:29:50.370 --> 00:30:17.100
<v Jack Geiger>You know, what occurs to me now is something I learned in the Harlem community and in a lot of other work. There was a lot more experience in the black community of extended families. And I don't think, in that context, from that side of the divide, it felt like such a big deal.
<v Jack Geiger>你知道，我现在脑子里浮现的是我在哈林区社区以及很多其他工作中学到的一些东西。黑人社区里有着更丰富的大家庭生活经验。而且我认为，在这种语境下，从分歧的另一边来看，这并不算什么大不了的事。

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:25.770
<v Ira Glass>Well, you're saying, in a way, that black culture at that time was more conducive to extending a kindness to strangers than white culture.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，你的意思是，在某种程度上，当时的黑人文化比白人文化更倾向于向陌生人施以善意。

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:37.010
<v Jack Geiger>I think so. And maybe still.
<v Jack Geiger>我想是的。而且现在可能依然如此。

00:30:37.010 --> 00:30:59.040
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Jack Geiger in New York. An interesting footnote to this story-- in 1949, just a few years after he befriended Jack Geiger, Canada Lee was in a movie where he did more or less the same thing. The film was Lost Boundaries . He played an African American police officer who befriends a confused, white teenager, takes him under his wing, shows him the kindness of strangers.
<v Ira Glass>杰克·盖格医生在纽约。这个故事的一个有趣花絮是——在1949年，就在他与杰克·盖格结为好友几年后，卡纳达·李出演了一部电影，他在片中扮演了差不多的角色。这部电影是《迷失的边界》（Lost Boundaries）。他扮演了一名非裔美国警察，与一个迷茫的白人少年交朋友，对其实施庇护，向他展现了陌生人的善意。

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:13.710
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, good neighbors and bad neighbors in the same neighborhood, a street mob, a tap dancer, a PA system, and the Chairman of the Board. That's in a minute from Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，同一个社区里的好邻居与坏邻居、街头暴徒、踢踏舞者、广播系统以及董事会主席。节目继续，公共广播国际（Public Radio International）稍后为您播出。

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:29.590
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, the kindness of strangers and where the kindness might lead. All of our stories in today's show are from the supposedly least kind city in America, New York City.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《这个美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。当然，我们每周都会在节目中选定一个主题，为您带来各种不同类型的相关故事。今天的节目主题是：陌生人的善意，以及这份善意会将我们带向何方。今天节目中的所有故事都发生在美国据称最缺少善意的城市——纽约市。

00:31:29.590 --> 00:31:53.400
<v Ira Glass>And we have arrived at Act Three of our program. And for this act, we figured-- nice, nice, nice, nice, nice. We figured we would need a change of pace after all this kindness and attempted kindness. And this is a story about the flat-out unkindness of strangers and how it could take two people who do not know each other and make them completely obsessed with each other. Paul Tough reports. Some names in this story have been changed.
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了节目的第三幕。对于这一幕，我们觉得——好，好，好，好，好。我们觉得在经历了这么多善意和尝试行善之后，我们需要转换一下节奏。这是一个关于陌生人之间赤裸裸的恶意，以及它如何能让两个原本互不相识的人对彼此彻底痴迷的故事。保罗·陶夫（Paul Tough）报道。本故事中的部分姓名已作修改。

00:31:53.400 --> 00:32:17.780
<v Paul Tough>Helga's neighborhood used to be entirely Ukrainian, respectable with an Orthodox Church and a community center. And then things changed. Then people started arriving. And now the place is full of record stores and cafes and body piercing parlors. Starlee is one of the newcomers. She moved in two years ago, right next door to Helga. And the trouble between them started right away. Starlee says that at first it was just regular New York apartment stuff.
<v Paul Tough>赫尔加（Helga）的社区过去完全是乌克兰人的聚居区，体面而正派，有一座东正教教堂和一个社区中心。然后一切都变了。然后人们开始搬进来。现在这里到处都是唱片店、咖啡馆和人体穿刺店。斯塔莉（Starlee）就是新来者之一。她两年前搬进来，就住在赫尔加隔壁。他们之间的摩擦立刻就开始了。斯塔莉说，起初这只是一些纽约公寓的寻常琐事。

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:26.440
<v Starlee Kine>She would come and tell me not to make noise in the apartment. And she was like, I wear slippers at night, so you should be wearing slippers too.
<v Starlee Kine>她会来告诉我不要在公寓里制造噪音。她就像是，我晚上都穿拖鞋，所以你也应该穿拖鞋。

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:28.270
<v Paul Tough>And she'd come and just knock on your door and tell you that?
<v Paul Tough>她会特地跑来敲你的门然后对你说这些吗？

00:32:28.270 --> 00:32:45.450
<v Starlee Kine>No, she would tell me downstairs in the hall. I'd see her in passing. And she was actually really calm about it. And she looked like a helpless old woman back then. And I'd try to be quieter because of it. And I think I even helped her carry her groceries up the stairs once. I think I actually did do that.
<v Starlee Kine>不，她会在楼下的走廊里跟我说。我路过时会碰见她。而且她当时其实表现得非常冷静。那时她看起来就像一个无助的老妇人。因此我试着变得安静一些。我想我甚至还帮她把杂货搬上过楼梯。我想我确实这么做过。

00:32:45.450 --> 00:33:02.380
<v Paul Tough>From Starlee's point of view, she tried to be quiet. She tried to be nice. But she was a college student at the time, and she had a lot of friends. And people would drop by late at night. So it was hard to be quiet all the time. From Helga's point of view, Starlee was a terrible neighbor, the worst. And Helga made sure that Starlee knew exactly how she felt.
<v Paul Tough>从斯塔莉的角度来看，她努力保持安静。她努力表现得友善。但她当时是个大学生，有很多朋友。人们会在深夜来访。所以要一直保持安静是很困难的。从赫尔加的角度来看，斯塔莉是个糟糕的邻居，糟糕透顶。赫尔加确保斯塔莉清楚地知道她是怎么想的。

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:18.330
<v Starlee Kine>She would occasionally sit in the hallway and talk to people about us. But there would not be anyone out there to talk to. She would just make up conversation and gossip about us. But we'd open the door and she'd be like, they all ran upstairs really quick. And she wasn't talking to anyone about us. Just so we'd know that she didn't like us.
<v Starlee Kine>她偶尔会坐在走廊里，跟别人谈论我们。但其实外面根本没有人。她只是凭空捏造我们的对话和八卦。但当我们打开门时，她就会说，他们刚刚飞快地跑上楼了。其实她根本没跟任何人谈论我们。她这么做只是想让我们知道她讨厌我们。

00:33:18.330 --> 00:33:20.380
<v Paul Tough>And so what sorts of things was she saying at that point?
<v Paul Tough>那当时她都在说些什么呢？

00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:36.090
<v Starlee Kine>Just that we were loud, bad kids. Just that we were loud and unresponsible. And she didn't believe any of us ever went to school. She refused to believe that. I think she didn't like that we were young.
<v Starlee Kine>就说我们是吵闹、糟糕的孩子。说我们又吵又没有责任感。她不相信我们当中有谁去上过学。她坚决不信。我想她是不喜欢我们还年轻吧。

00:33:36.090 --> 00:33:49.410
<v Paul Tough>Helga wanted everyone else in the building to see the Starlee that she saw. So she started throwing garbage out into the little landing that they shared, apparently to try to make everyone think that it was people in Starlee's apartment, number three, who were responsible.
<v Paul Tough>海尔加想让大楼里的其他人都看到她眼中的斯塔莉。于是她开始把垃圾扔到他们共用的那个小楼梯平台上，显然是想让大家以为，是斯塔莉的3号公寓里的人干的。

00:33:49.410 --> 00:34:27.810
<v Starlee Kine>Cigarette butts and crumpled pieces of paper and orange juice cartons. Stuff like that. And it started off really small and it just got huge. And it just became so much trash in the hallway. And people smoke here, so it looked like we were doing it. And also, the type of trash she picked-- she would try to go out of her way to find kid trash, like Hostess Donut wrappers and candy bar things, and just the most creative garbage you've ever seen. And so people at first thought we were doing it. And they would come and talk to us and be like, don't put the trash. I'm like, I'm not putting the trash.
<v Starlee Kine>烟头、揉皱的纸团和橙汁纸盒。诸如此类的东西。一开始还很少，后来变得越来越多。走廊里的垃圾多得不得了。而且这里的人抽烟，所以看起来就像是我们干的。而且，她挑的垃圾种类——她还会特意去找小孩子吃的垃圾，比如好蒂甜甜圈的包装纸和巧克力棒包装纸，那绝对是你见过最有创意的垃圾。所以大家一开始都以为是我们干的。他们会过来对我们说，别乱扔垃圾。我说，我根本没扔垃圾。

00:34:27.810 --> 00:34:36.800
<v Paul Tough>Jake Bronstein, Starlee's roommate at the time-- she's had nine roommates, I should say, nine, in the two years she's lived there-- Jake decided to do something.
<v Paul Tough>杰克·布朗斯坦，斯塔莉当时的室友——我应该说，在她住在那里的两年里，她换过九个室友，整整九个——杰克决定做点什么。

00:34:36.800 --> 00:35:01.690
<v Starlee Kine>Jake wrote a note saying, please don't put trash in the hallway and put it outside in our hallway. We have a little square hallway. And he just taped it on the wall. And so then we hear her come out and we look through the key hole. And we see that she's put a sign up. And we come out there and it says, well then please don't sell drugs. And that's the first time we'd ever heard of it. We were just like, whoa. It was so out of the blue. We couldn't believe it.
<v Starlee Kine>杰克写了一张便条，上面写着“请不要把垃圾扔在走廊里”，然后贴在我们走廊外面。我们有一个方形的小走廊。他只是把它贴在墙上。然后我们就听到她出来了，我们通过钥匙孔往外看。我们看到她贴了一张告示。我们走出去一看，上面写着：那请你们也别贩毒。那是我们第一次听说这事。当时我们都惊呆了。太突兀了。我们简直不敢相信。

00:35:01.690 --> 00:35:23.570
<v Paul Tough>Ever since that day more than six months ago, Helga's put up at least one note about Starlee every single day, sometimes as many as seven or eight. They're mostly pretty small, maybe two inches by three inches. The notes are written in marker in block letters. Helga puts notes on the front door, over the mailboxes inside, on the window, on her own door, on Starlee's door.
<v Paul Tough>从六个多月前的那个日子起，海尔加每天至少贴一张关于斯塔莉的便条，有时甚至多达七八张。它们通常都很小，大概两英寸乘三英寸。这些便条是用马克笔写的印刷体大写字母。海尔加把便条贴在前门上、里面信箱的上方、窗户上、她自己的门上、斯塔莉的门上。

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:40.140
<v Paul Tough>The wording varies but the message is always the same. Starlee is a big-time drug dealer, she's selling drugs out of apartment number three, and she should stop or move out. Starlee actually collects the notes that Helga puts up. One whole wall of her apartment is covered with them. Starlee shows me a few choice ones.
<v Paul Tough>措辞虽然不同，但传达的信息始终如一。斯塔莉是个大毒贩，她在3号公寓里贩毒，她应该住手或者搬走。斯塔莉其实把海尔加贴出的便条都收集了起来。她公寓整整一面墙都贴满了这些便条。斯塔莉给我看了几张比较有代表性的。

00:35:40.140 --> 00:36:07.940
<v Starlee Kine>See, she puts number three, selling drugs, business as usual. On Passover, she put, shame selling drugs on Passover. Let's see, Kine and Bronstein, drug dealers selling your way to the jailhouse. Kine and Bronstein, drug dealers selling illegal drugs here, like parasites.
<v Starlee Kine>你看，她写着3号房，正在贩毒，照常营业。在逾越节那天，她写着，在逾越节贩毒真可耻。让我看看，凯恩和布朗斯坦，贩毒把你们送进监狱。凯恩和布朗斯坦，像寄生虫一样在这里贩卖非法毒品。

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:15.080
<v Paul Tough>Starlee says that, in fact, she's not selling drugs. She's never sold drugs. No one in her apartment has ever sold drugs. It's all a big lie.
<v Paul Tough>斯塔莉说，事实上，她没有贩毒。她从来没有贩毒。她公寓里的任何人也从来没有贩毒过。这全都是弥天大谎。

00:36:15.080 --> 00:36:26.700
<v Starlee Kine>I asked her to come into the apartment. I'm like, come in the apartment, look anywhere you want. Honestly, we'll go to the cops together. I don't mind that at all.
<v Starlee Kine>我让她进公寓来。我说，进公寓来看看，随便你想看哪儿都行。老实说，我们一块儿去报警吧。我一点也不介意。

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:39.130
<v Paul Tough>Helga doesn't just put up signs. She appears at her door whenever anyone comes to visit Starlee. She harasses Starlee's friends, accosts Starlee in the hallway, calls her a liar. And then there's this.
<v Paul Tough>海尔加可不止是贴告示。每当有人来探望斯塔莉时，她就会出现在自家门口。她骚扰斯塔莉的朋友，在走廊里纠缠斯塔莉，骂她是骗子。然后还有这个。

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:39.630
<v Paul Tough>[TAPPING]
<v Paul Tough>[敲击声]

00:36:39.600 --> 00:36:42.410
<v Starlee Kine>Now it's over there, though. See? It moves.
<v Starlee Kine>不过现在敲到那边去了。看？它在移动。

00:36:42.410 --> 00:37:03.650
<v Paul Tough>We're inside Starlee's apartment, about 1:00 in the morning. Helga is sitting in her apartment, right next to the thin wall that separates the two of them. And she's tapping on the floor, just to let Starlee know she's there. She's always watching. I look up at the wall. For me, it's a very creepy moment. Starlee's used to it.
<v Paul Tough>我们在斯塔莉的公寓里，大约凌晨1点。海尔加正坐在她自己的公寓里，紧挨着把他们隔开的那堵薄墙。她正敲击着地板，只是为了让斯塔莉知道她在。她一直在监视着。我抬头看着墙壁。对我来说，这是一个非常诡异的时刻。斯塔莉已经习以为常了。

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:09.710
<v Paul Tough>I got to say, it's the--
<v Paul Tough>我得说，这真是……

00:37:09.710 --> 00:37:20.970
<v Starlee Kine>It's just to get our attention, just to remind us that even when she's not putting the signs up that the she's aware of our illegal activities.
<v Starlee Kine>这只是为了引起我们的注意，只是为了提醒我们，即使她没有贴告示，她也知道我们的非法活动。

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:23.800
<v Starlee Kine>[TAPPING]
<v Starlee Kine>id: 136|||[敲击声]

00:37:23.800 --> 00:37:44.370
<v Starlee Kine>We watched I, Claudius on PBS and it was three hours. From the first credit to the last credit, it was continuous tapping the entire time. She just sat in her door and banged her cane for the entire time. And she'll do it. She does not get tired. And that's what she does with her day. Instead of eating, she just bangs her cane.
<v Starlee Kine>我们在公共电视网看《我，克劳狄乌斯》，播了三个小时。从第一个片名字幕到最后一个片名字幕，敲击声整整持续了三个小时。她就坐在门边，不停地敲着她的手杖。而且她会一直这么干。她根本不会累。这就是她一天都在做的事。她不吃饭，只是敲手杖。

00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:45.300
<v Paul Tough>That's kind of sad.
<v Paul Tough>这有点可悲。

00:37:45.300 --> 00:38:15.490
<v Starlee Kine>It is sad. I know it's sad. It's hard, though. Because it's hard to know what the right thing to do is and to be confronted by such meanness. Sometimes you'll just be coming home, and you'll be like, god, this woman is the saddest woman in the world. You'll pass by her door and get feelings of pity and affection. And then she'll open it and she'll yell at you. And you're just like, man. She makes it so hard to do the right-- to just be a good person about it.
<v Starlee Kine>是很悲哀。我知道这很可悲。不过这很难熬。因为很难知道什么是正确该做的事，而且还要面对如此刻薄的对待。有时你刚回到家，就会想，天哪，这个女人是世界上最可悲的女人。你从她门前走过时，会产生怜悯和喜爱之情。然后她会把门打开，冲你大吼大叫。你会觉得，天哪。她把事情搞得那么难办，让人很难去做对的事——很难为此做一个好人。

00:38:15.490 --> 00:38:43.000
<v Paul Tough>A couple of years ago, there actually was a drug dealer in the building up on the top floor. It was a bad scene. Junkies being dragged downstairs and out to the front stoop, people sleeping on the roof. And everyone else in the building banded together and went to court and actually got the drug dealers kicked out. Helga was one of people who testified. And Starlee thinks that that might be connected to what's happening now. Helga got a lot of attention and support from that campaign. And so now she's trying to do it again with Starlee.
<v Paul Tough>几年前，大楼顶层其实真的住过一个毒贩。那场面真糟糕。瘾君子们被拖到楼下一楼的门廊，有人直接睡在屋顶上。大楼里的其他人联合起来去了法院，真的把那些毒贩给赶走了。海尔加就是作证的人之一。斯塔莉认为这可能跟现在发生的事情有关。海尔加从那场行动中获得了大量的关注和支持。所以现在她想在斯塔莉身上故伎重施。

00:38:43.000 --> 00:39:03.140
<v Paul Tough>I tried to speak to Helga about all of this, to get her side of the story, but it wasn't easy. The way Starlee described her, she's suspicious of strangers. She never lets anyone into her apartment. She doesn't answer her buzzer. So I decided that I'd try to speak to her out on the street. One night I waited outside the building for about an hour. And finally, she came out.
<v Paul Tough>我曾试图跟海尔加谈谈这一切，听听她那边的说法，但这并不容易。正如斯塔莉所描述的那样，她对陌生人充满戒备。她从不让任何人进入她的公寓。她连门禁蜂鸣器都不应答。所以我决定试着在街上跟她谈谈。有一天晚上我在大楼外面等了一个多小时。最后，她总算出来了。

00:39:03.140 --> 00:39:07.980
<v Paul Tough>Excuse me. Do you live in this building here? I'm trying to find out--
<v Paul Tough>打扰一下。你住这栋楼吗？我正想打听一下……

00:39:07.980 --> 00:39:30.720
<v Ira Glass>It was a very strange interview. She wasn't what I expected. She seemed completely normal. I told her that I had heard something was going on with apartment number three, and I asked her if she knew it was. Yes, she told me, they're selling drugs. I asked her if she'd talk to me about, and she said that she would, but she didn't want me to use her voice on the radio. Too dangerous, she said.
<v Ira Glass>这是一次非常奇怪的采访。她跟我想象的完全不一样。她看起来完全正常。我告诉她我听说3号公寓出了点事，我问她知不知道是怎么回事。是的，她告诉我，他们在贩毒。我问她愿不愿意跟我聊聊这个，她说她愿意，但她不希望我在广播里使用她的声音。太危险了，她说。

00:39:30.720 --> 00:40:08.450
<v Ira Glass>She led me across the street, behind a van, where she said it would be safer to talk. She was deadly serious, very intense. She clearly felt that she was in a dangerous situation. She was willing to give me a few details, but the rest, she told me, I'd have to dig out myself. Here's what she said. The building is full of students. The people in apartment three sell to the students in the building. They also use the students as couriers to sell drugs in the bars all around the neighborhood. It's a big operation, and it's all being run by that short girl, she said, meaning Starlee.
<v Ira Glass>她把我带到街对面，一辆货车后面，她说在那里说话更安全。她神情极其严肃，非常紧张。很显然，她觉得自己正处于危险的境地。她愿意透露一些细节，但剩下的，她告诉我，得让我自己去挖掘。她是这么说的。这栋楼里住满了学生。3号公寓的人把毒品卖给楼里的学生。他们还利用学生作为信使，把毒品卖到整个社区的酒吧里。这是一项大买卖，而且全部是由那个矮个女孩操纵的，她说，指的是斯塔莉。

00:40:08.450 --> 00:40:47.730
<v Ira Glass>If Helga were to hear this story on the radio, she would tell you that I've got it all wrong, that I've been duped, that everything Starlee told me is a lie. And some of what Helga says makes perfect sense. She says that people are coming and going all the time from Starlee's apartment, which is, in fact, true. She says the phone rings at all hours of the night, and it does. For Helga, that points to one thing, drugs. For Starlee, it's just that she's a student. She stays up late. She's got a lot of friends. There's no middle ground for Starlee and Helga. They see absolutely everything differently.
<v Ira Glass>如果海尔加在广播里听到这个故事，她会告诉你我全弄错了，我被骗了，斯塔莉跟我说的一切都是谎言。海尔加所说的一些话确实很有道理。她说有人频繁进出斯塔莉的公寓，这其实是真的。她说电话在深夜随时会响，确实如此。对海尔加来说，这只指向一件事：毒品。对斯塔莉来说，这只是因为她是个学生。她睡得很晚。她有很多朋友。对斯塔莉和海尔加来说，根本没有中间地带。她们对一切事物的看法完全截然不同。

00:40:47.730 --> 00:40:58.940
<v Ira Glass>From Starlee's point of view, there was a period at the beginning where this whole thing was sort of funny, where it was just a good story. But as time went on, things changed. And Starlee became as serious as Helga.
<v Ira Glass>在斯塔莉看来，有一段时间，整件事其实还有点搞笑，觉得这只是个好故事。但随着时间的推移，情况变了。斯塔莉变得和海尔加一样严肃。

00:40:58.940 --> 00:41:15.620
<v Starlee Kine>There would be times when I wanted to catch her in the act so badly, because I never caught-- she's so quick. And you could never catch her putting the signs up. I wanted to just open it so badly that I just wouldn't leave for an hour. I'd be like, no, you guys go ahead to the movie. I'm just going to stay here and just wait by the door for a little longer. And it was hard.
<v Starlee Kine>有时我太想抓她个正着了，因为我从来没抓到过——她太敏捷了。你永远抓不到她贴告示。我太想把门打开了，以至于在一个小时内都不肯离开。我会想，不，你们去看电影吧。我只想留在这儿，在门边再多等一会儿。这真的很煎熬。

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:24.270
<v Paul Tough>There's a way, especially at that period, where the two of you were sort of inextricably linked. That she's sitting there waiting in her apartment for you, and you're sitting here--
<v Paul Tough>有那么一段时间，尤其是那个时期，你们两个人的命运似乎紧紧缠绕在了一起。她在公寓里坐着等你的出现，而你坐在这里……

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:39.750
<v Starlee Kine>Right. Well, that's what it was. It was me lying in wait of her lying in wait for me. Absolutely. Yeah, the bond was strong.
<v Starlee Kine>没错。嗯，事实就是这样。就是我在潜伏等待她潜伏等待我。绝对是这样。是的，这种羁绊很深。

00:41:39.750 --> 00:42:09.470
<v Paul Tough>Most of the time, the unkindness of strangers is a barely conscious thing. You cut someone off in traffic. You take the last doughnut. You bump into someone running for a train. You don't even think about it. With Helga and Starlee, things are different. They're unkind to each other, they spy on each other, they bicker, they yell at each other in the hallway. But for each of them, those unkindnesses are part of a bigger picture. They're mean because they have to be. Starlee's trying to clear her name. Helga's trying to clean up the neighborhood.
<v Paul Tough>大多数时候，陌生人的恶意只是一种隐隐约约的意识。你在交通堵塞时强行加塞。你拿走了最后一个甜甜圈。你赶火车时撞到了别人。你甚至都不会去想它。但对于海尔加和斯塔莉来说，事情完全不同。她们对彼此不友善，她们互相监视，她们争吵，她们在走廊里冲彼此大喊大叫。但对她们每个人来说，这些恶意都是更宏大画卷的一部分。她们刻薄是因为她们觉得不得不如此。斯塔莉在努力澄清自己的名誉。海尔加在努力整顿社区环境。

00:42:09.470 --> 00:42:32.480
<v Starlee Kine>If someone had gotten to her first-- if you were writing a history of the East Village or writing the history of this building, and then someone just interviewed her, it would go down that this heroic old lady tried to get these drug dealers out. She'd be the martyr, or whatever. And I guess that could become fact then. I told you, it almost is fact sometimes. I'm questioning myself sometimes about it. I used to.
<v Starlee Kine>如果有人先找到了她——如果你在写东村的历史或这栋楼的历史，然后有人去采访她，历史就会记载这位英勇的老太太试图把这些毒贩赶出去。她会成为殉道者，或者诸如此类的人物。我想这到头来就会变成既定事实了。我跟你说过，有时候它几乎就成了事实。有时连我都在自我怀疑。我以前经常这样。

00:42:32.480 --> 00:42:34.880
<v Paul Tough>You used to question whether or not you were a drug dealer?
<v Paul Tough>你以前曾怀疑过自己到底是不是毒贩？

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:45.430
<v Starlee Kine>No, but just am I right? Am I doing something wrong? Is there something wrong I'm doing like that? Is she a little bit right? Not that I'm a drug dealer, but am I--
<v Starlee Kine>不，只是怀疑我是不是对的？我做错什么了吗？我做的事有什么不对劲吗？她是不是有点道理？倒不是说我是个毒贩，而是我……

00:42:45.430 --> 00:42:47.570
<v Paul Tough>Like just are you a bad neighbor?
<v Paul Tough>就像你是个糟糕的邻居？

00:42:47.570 --> 00:42:52.720
<v Starlee Kine>Yeah, bad neighbor, bad person. Am I abusing her?
<v Starlee Kine>对，糟糕的邻居，糟糕的人。我在虐待她吗？

00:42:52.720 --> 00:43:09.300
<v Paul Tough>Starlee always thought of herself as a basically good and neighborly person. She never thought she was the kind of person who would do something like yell at an old woman in the hall. And yet she does. Unkindness breeds unkindness. Still, Starlee can't help wishing that things could somehow be different.
<v Paul Tough>斯塔莉一直认为自己基本上是个善良、有邻里情谊的人。她从没想过自己会是那种在走廊里冲老太太大喊大叫的人。然而她确实这么做了。恶言相向只会滋生恶言。尽管如此，斯塔莉还是忍不住希望事情能有所不同。

00:43:09.300 --> 00:43:47.230
<v Starlee Kine>I've been having these dreams-- very, very clear dreams, like long, epic dreams-- where she'd come over, and we've chatted on the bed, and we've been giggling in the dreams. And I've had dreams where we've come to terms with a lot of things. I've explained it. Like, this summer. Before, they used to be-- they were only violent. I was at her funeral. I was a little sad at that point. I was visiting her in jail. But this time, I've had these friendship dreams all summer long.
<v Starlee Kine>我一直在做梦——非常非常清晰的梦，就像那种漫长而史诗般的梦——在梦里她会过来，我们在床边聊天，在梦里咯咯地笑。我还做过我们达成某种和解的梦。我都解释清楚了。就像这个夏天。以前，那些梦总是——只有暴力场面。我参加她的葬礼。当时我有点难过。我去监狱探望她。但这一次，整个夏天我都梦到我们成了朋友。

00:43:47.230 --> 00:44:06.850
<v Starlee Kine>And they're really realistic. She acts like herself and I say the thing I'm supposed to. I can't figure out what I'm supposed to say when I'm awake. And I say it. And then all of a sudden, really, logic comes into her eyes. And she has sat down on my bed, and we've started to giggle and just talk about things and make jokes to each other.
<v Starlee Kine>而且它们非常逼真。她的表现还是她自己，而我说出了我应该说的话。我清醒的时候老是想不出我该说些什么。但我在梦里说出来了。然后突然之间，理智真的出现在她的眼中。她坐在我的床边，我们开始咯咯笑着谈论事情，互相开玩笑。

00:44:06.850 --> 00:44:10.040
<v Paul Tough>What do you mean, the thing that you're supposed to say that you can't figure out?
<v Paul Tough>你说的“你该说却想不出来的话”是什么意思？

00:44:10.040 --> 00:44:20.790
<v Starlee Kine>Whatever I'm supposed to be saying, whatever I could possibly say to her in real life to make her see the light, you know.
<v Starlee Kine>不管我应该说些什么，不管我在现实生活中究竟能对她说些什么才能让她幡然醒悟，你知道的。

00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:27.000
<v Paul Tough>Well, how do you think you'd feel if the notes just suddenly stopped one day?
<v Paul Tough>好吧，如果有一天便条突然停了，你觉得你会有什么感觉？

00:44:27.000 --> 00:44:37.370
<v Starlee Kine>I don't know. I'd probably wait a couple days and I'd see-- I don't know. I can't imagine they would stop, though.
<v Starlee Kine>我不知道。我大概会等上几天看看吧——我不知道。不过我无法想象它们会停下来。

00:44:37.370 --> 00:44:39.210
<v Paul Tough>So when was the last time you talked to her?
<v Paul Tough>那么你最后一次跟她说话是什么时候？

00:44:39.210 --> 00:44:51.490
<v Starlee Kine>I talked to her yesterday. She called me a pathological liar.
<v Starlee Kine>我昨天跟她说过话。她骂我是个病态的骗子。

00:44:51.490 --> 00:45:06.890
<v Starlee Kine>[MUSIC - "LOVE TO ANNOY" BY JULIE DOIRON]
<v Starlee Kine>[音乐 - 朱莉·多伊龙的《热衷烦人》]

00:45:06.890 --> 00:45:21.910
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Chairman of the Block. This story takes place almost around the corner from Starlee's apartment building, just a few blocks away. It's about one small act of kindness leading somewhere completely unexpected. A resident of the neighborhood, Blake Eskin, tells the story.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，街区主席。这个故事发生在离斯塔莉公寓大楼不远的街角，仅隔几个街区。它讲述了一个小小的善举如何引向完全意想不到的结果。社区居民布莱克·埃斯金讲述了这个故事。

00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:33.730
<v Blake Eskin>About a month ago, I went out one Friday evening with a friend in the East Village, where we both live. On the street, we heard Frank Sinatra music blasting loud enough to wake the neighbors.
<v Blake Eskin>大约一个月前的一个周五晚上，我和一位朋友一起去了我们共同居住的东村。在街上，我们听到弗兰克·辛纳屈的音乐声震耳欲聋，足以唤醒邻居。

00:45:33.730 --> 00:45:39.720
<v Nick Drakides>[SINGING "YOUNG AT HEART"]
<v Nick Drakides>[演唱《年轻的心》]

00:45:39.720 --> 00:46:04.970
<v Blake Eskin>As we reached Fourth Street, I saw 100 people huddled around the stoop of a sixth floor tenement. Most of them were post-college, pre-childbearing types. Plus there were some older people who probably lived on the block. Everyone seemed to have forgotten where they were headed, whether to a party or to another bar or back to bed.
<v Blake Eskin>当我们走到第四街时，我看到100人聚集在一栋六层公寓楼的门廊周围。他们大多是大学毕业后、生育之前的群体。此外还有一些可能住在该街区的老年人。每个人似乎都忘了自己要去哪里，是去参加派对、去另一家酒吧，还是回床睡觉。

00:46:04.970 --> 00:46:40.240
<v Blake Eskin>A short, dark-haired guy in a suit stood at the top of the stoop holding a microphone. At first, I thought maybe the guy was lip syncing because he sounded exactly like Sinatra. But after a few seconds, I realized he was doing the crooning himself. The guy looked a little like Sinatra, and he moved like him too. But this was no run-of-the-mill Sinatra impersonator. It was as if he was possessed by the spirit of Sinatra, channeling the Chairman of the Board, that Frank himself had emerged from retirement, dyed his hair black again, and was with us on Fourth Street.
<v Blake Eskin>一个穿着西装、留着黑短发的小个子男人站在门廊顶上，手里拿着麦克风。起初，我还以为那家伙是在对口型，因为他的声音听起来跟辛纳屈一模一样。但几秒钟后，我意识到他是在自己轻声慢唱。那家伙看起来有点像辛纳屈，他的动作也跟辛纳屈很像。但这绝不是那种平庸的辛纳屈模仿秀。这就好像他被辛纳屈的灵魂附体，化身为主席阁下本人，仿佛弗兰克本人已经复出，把头发重新染成黑色，和我们一起来到了第四街。

00:46:40.240 --> 00:46:53.470
<v Nick Drakides>[SINGING "THE LADY IS A TRAMP"] Come over here, Susan!
<v Nick Drakides>[演唱《荡妇》] 过来这儿，苏珊！

00:46:53.470 --> 00:47:32.000
<v Blake Eskin>At the bottom of the stoop was someone you would not ordinarily see with Frank Sinatra. An older woman with spiky salt-and-pepper hair and a leopard print vest was doing a spirited if slightly awkward tap dance on a piece of wood she had dragged out onto the sidewalk.
<v Blake Eskin>门廊底部站着一个你通常不会跟弗兰克·辛纳屈联系在一起的人。一位头发花白、根根竖起、穿着豹纹马甲的老年妇女，正踩在一块她拖到人行道上的木板上，跳着精神抖擞但略显笨拙的踢踏舞。

00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:58.370
<v Blake Eskin>After my initial confusion, and my subsequent bliss, my next reaction was to wonder how this was possible. Where were the cops? The 9th Precinct is a block away, and New Yorkers are quick to complain about noise. And Mayor Rudolph Giuliani has made it a priority for the police to crack down on what he calls "quality of life violations" like these, noise, crowds, blocking traffic, drinking in the street.
<v Blake Eskin>在我最初的困惑和随之而来的狂喜之后，我的下一个反应是想知道这怎么可能。警察在哪里？第九分局就在一个街区外，而且纽约人对噪音抱怨得很快。鲁迪·朱利安尼市长一直把打击他所谓的“生活质量违规行为”作为警方的首要任务，比如噪音、人群、堵塞交通、在街头饮酒。

00:47:58.370 --> 00:48:38.040
<v Blake Eskin>But on Fourth Street, everything was copasetic. And it still is. Somehow, by some quirk of fate, the show outside 124 East Fourth Street has happened five Fridays in a row. The singer, Nick Drakides, lives on the first floor of the building. And the tap dancer, Laraine Goodman, lives on four. Gary and Wanda, who run the garden-level thrift shop, put their merchandise, the chairs and overstuffed couches, on the sidewalk for the audience's comfort.
<v Blake Eskin>但在第四街上，一切都好得很。现在依然如此。不知怎么的，由于命运的巧合，东第四街124号门外的演出已经连续举办了五个星期五。歌手尼克·德拉基德斯住在该建筑的一楼。而踢踏舞者拉兰·古德曼住在四楼。经营底层旧货店的加里和旺达把他们的商品、椅子和软垫沙发放在人行道上，以供观众舒适地坐着。

00:48:38.040 --> 00:49:03.530
<v Blake Eskin>Nick Drakides and Laraine Goodman are neighbors. And like most people who live in the same building, they didn't know much about each other. Laraine did know, however, that Nick had a big jazz record collection. Five weeks ago, Laraine decided she wanted to tap dance in front of the building, as a sort of therapy, she says. And she reached out to Nick, asking him to play some tunes while she tap danced that weekend.
<v Blake Eskin>尼克·德拉基德斯和拉兰·古德曼是邻居。就像大多数住在同一栋楼里的人一样，他们对彼此并不太了解。不过，拉兰确实知道尼克有很多爵士乐唱片收藏。五个星期前，拉兰决定在建筑物前跳踢踏舞，用她的话说，这算是一种疗法。她联系了尼克，请他在那个周末她跳踢踏舞时放一些曲子。

00:49:03.530 --> 00:49:27.100
<v Nick Drakides>What happened was, I was coming home-- I'll tell you exactly what happened. I was coming home that Friday evening around 9:00 and I forgot her name. And I'm walking down Fourth Street from Second Avenue. And I'm like, oh, there she is tapping, and I don't want to do this. I'm tired. I'm like [SIGH]. And then I had to reach for her name in my little-- what's this thing-- pocket day timer. And I'm like, OK, it's Laraine.
<v Nick Drakides>事情是这样的，我当时正走在回家的路上——我来告诉你到底发生了什么。那个周五晚上9点左右我正走在回家的路上，我忘了她的名字。我正沿着第四街从第二大道走过来。我想，噢，她在那儿跳舞呢，我可不想管这事。我很累。我想着[叹气]。然后我不得不去我的小本本——这叫什么来着——口袋日记本里找她的名字。我想，好吧，是拉兰。

00:49:27.100 --> 00:49:42.670
<v Nick Drakides>Then I walk down the street, and I say, hi, Laraine, how are you? And she goes, oh come on out Nick and join me, blah, blah, blah. And I think she assumed I'll bring out some music. That was it. I don't think she was expecting a suit and microphone stand and the PA, the CDs, the cassettes, the whole number.
<v Nick Drakides>然后我走下街道，我说，嗨，拉兰，你好吗？她说，噢，尼克，快来加入我，巴拉巴拉巴拉。我想她以为我会带点音乐出来。就那样。我想她一定没想到我会穿西装、带麦克风架、PA音响、CD、磁带，整套装备全上场。

00:49:42.670 --> 00:49:51.520
<v Nick Drakides>Thanks to Laraine Goodman. This is the brains behind this wonderful event here. Say good evening, Laraine.
<v Nick Drakides>感谢拉兰·古德曼。这是这里这场精彩活动幕后的策划者。说声晚上好，拉兰。

00:49:51.520 --> 00:49:53.630
<v Laraine Goodman>Good evening, Laraine.
<v Laraine Goodman>大家晚上好，我是拉兰。

00:49:53.630 --> 00:50:19.630
<v Blake Eskin>Nick's initial gesture of kindness to Laraine, a near stranger, made her into a local celebrity and made himself into an even bigger one. There were only a handful of people watching Laraine tap dance when Nick went outside with his instant Sinatra kit, which includes a few CDs from a series called Pocket Songs. The discs have the full Sinatra arrangements without a vocalist. The slogan is "You Sing the Hits."
<v Blake Eskin>尼克对拉兰这个几乎是陌生人的人最初的善举，使她成了当地的名人，也让自己变得更加有名。当尼克带着他的“即兴弗兰克·辛纳屈全套装备”（其中包括几张名为《口袋歌曲》系列的CD）走到室外时，只有少数人在看拉兰跳踢踏舞。这些光盘没有主唱，只有完整的辛纳屈管弦乐编曲。其口号是“你来唱热门金曲”。

00:50:19.630 --> 00:50:32.110
<v Blake Eskin>Nick began with "I've Got the World on a String." The crowd built steadily. And right away, Nick had the crowd on a string, standing on the stoop, had the string around his finger. What a world.
<v Blake Eskin>尼克以《我把整个世界握在手中》开场。人群稳步聚集。立刻，尼克就把人群紧紧吸引住了，大家站在门廊上，他把人群攥在了手心。多么美妙的世界。

00:50:32.110 --> 00:50:42.630
<v Nick Drakides>[SINGING "I'VE GOT THE WORLD ON A STRING"]
<v Nick Drakides>[演唱《我把整个世界握在手中》]

00:50:42.630 --> 00:51:00.040
<v Blake Eskin>Nick showed me a picture taken when he was 15. He's wearing a tuxedo, his hair parted to the side, standing at a microphone and pointing back at the camera. It is a picture of a 15-year-old boy from Poughkeepsie, New York in Frank Sinatra drag.
<v Blake Eskin>尼克给我看了一张他15岁时拍的照片。他穿着燕尾服，头发偏分，站在麦克风前，指着身后的相机。这是一张来自纽约波基浦西的15岁男孩模仿弗兰克·辛纳屈的照片。

00:51:00.040 --> 00:51:21.390
<v Nick Drakides>Basically, what I'm doing right now, I have been into since I was a kid, since I was 10 years old.
<v Nick Drakides>基本上，我现在在做的事情，我从还是个孩子的时候就开始迷恋了，从我10岁起。

00:51:21.390 --> 00:51:41.410
<v Blake Eskin>Nick trained as a jazz vocalist at Boston's Berklee College of Music, moved to New York, and after a while he found a job with the Starlight Orchestra, a 16-piece band that performs at high society weddings and corporate events. The Starlight Orchestra has five vocalists, and Nick is their Sinatra specialist.
<v Blake Eskin>尼克曾在波士顿伯克利音乐学院接受爵士声乐训练，后来搬到纽约，不久后他在星光乐队找到了一份工作，这是一支由16人组成的乐队，在高级社会婚礼和公司活动中表演。星光乐队有五名主唱，尼克是他们的辛纳屈专职歌手。

00:51:41.410 --> 00:52:05.700
<v Blake Eskin>Each of us in the audience had been lured by the improbability of the situation, but Nick's stage presence kept us there. Most street performers in New York go where the tourists go, since most of us natives are too busy to stop and listen. Nick singing from his stoop, however, was a gift to his own neighborhood. Nick really knows how to work a room, even when it's not a room. He weaves his neighbor's names into the lyrics.
<v Blake Eskin>我们观众中的每一个人都被这种不可思议的场景所吸引，但尼克的台风把我们留在了那里。纽约大多数街头艺人都去游客去的地方，因为我们大多数本地人都太忙了，没空停下来听。然而，尼克在自家门廊上唱歌，却是送给他自己社区的一份礼物。尼克真的很擅长调动全场气氛，即使这里根本不是一个房间。他把邻居的名字编进了歌词里。

00:52:05.700 --> 00:52:18.890
<v Nick Drakides>--anytime he moves his-- that's Brendan, our lovely neighbor here. Lucky me-- how ya doin' Richie-- can't you see I'm in love?
<v Nick Drakides>——每当他动他的——那是布伦丹，我们这里可爱的邻居。我真幸运——里奇你好啊——难道你没看见我恋爱了吗？

00:52:18.890 --> 00:52:33.140
<v Blake Eskin>He plugs Gary and Wanda's thrift shop and thanks them for their help. He salutes a couple watching from a nearby fire escape. He dedicates "Witchcraft" to a pretty blonde standing in the back row and flirts with her at the end of the song.
<v Blake Eskin>他顺便宣传了加里和旺达的旧货店，并感谢他们的帮助。他向在附近防火梯上观看的一对夫妇致意。他将《巫术》献给站在最后一排的一个漂亮金发女郎，并在歌曲结束时和她调情。

00:52:33.140 --> 00:52:42.160
<v Nick Drakides>[SINGING "WITCHCRAFT"] Ooh! I got a crush on you too, baby. Ooh, you're a fine witch!
<v Nick Drakides>[演唱《巫术》] 噢！我对你也动心了，宝贝。噢，你是个迷人的小妖精！

00:52:42.160 --> 00:53:02.430
<v Blake Eskin>Just like Frank would have done. Now, it's a safe bet that if Nick and Laraine had been break dancing or playing conga drums, the police would have shut them down in 20 minutes, tops. But the officers of the 9th Precinct fell under the same spell as the rest of us. And they couldn't bring themselves to get out of the patrol car to enforce the mayor's quality of life rules.
<v Blake Eskin>就像弗兰克会做的那样。现在，可以肯定的是，如果尼克和拉兰当时在跳街舞或敲康加鼓，警察最多20分钟就会让他们收摊。但第九分局的警官们和我们其他人一样着了迷。他们实在无法说服自己从巡逻车里出来执行市长的生活质量规则。

00:53:02.430 --> 00:53:20.950
<v Nick Drakides>The first week they would circle around the block, speak through their megaphone. They would say, people, please don't block the streets. Please keep the streets clear. And that was it. That was the first week. The second week they requested "Summer Wind."
<v Nick Drakides>第一周，他们在街区周围转圈，通过扩音器喊话。他们会说，各位，请不要堵塞街道。请保持街道畅通。就这样。那是第一周。第二周他们点播了《夏日之风》。

00:53:20.950 --> 00:53:23.510
<v Blake Eskin>They requested "Summer Wind" through the megaphone?
<v Blake Eskin>他们通过扩音器点播了《夏日之风》？

00:53:23.510 --> 00:54:09.240
<v Nick Drakides>Yes, through the megaphone as they were passing. The third week, the police came and they stopped their car, held up traffic, and they said, OK, "Summer Wind." They wanted to hear "Summer Wind." So I finished "Night and Day." I put "Summer Wind" on. And I went up on the steps. They manipulated their lights on the top and threw a white spotlight on me. And I started singing "Summer Wind." The crowd went crazy. They went nuts. And they were really into it. it's that whole New York, macho Italian, police, Irish, street-- it is, man. And evidently, what I'm doing, they connect with that.
<v Nick Drakides>是的，当他们路过时通过扩音器点的。第三周，警察来了，他们停下车，阻挡了交通，他们说，好吧，《夏日之风》。他们想听《夏日之风》。所以我唱完了《黑夜与白天》。我放了《夏日之风》。我走到台阶上。他们操作着顶部的警灯，把白色的聚光灯投射在我身上。我开始唱《夏日之风》。人群疯狂了。他们彻底疯狂了。他们真的完全沉浸其中。这就是那种纽约式的、硬汉意大利风格、警察、爱尔兰风格、街头风格——就是这样，老兄。显然，我所做的事，他们能产生共鸣。

00:54:09.240 --> 00:54:17.800
<v Nick Drakides>[SINGING "SUMMER WIND"]
<v Nick Drakides>[演唱《夏日之风》]

00:54:17.800 --> 00:55:06.670
<v Blake Eskin>Of course they do. So do the black men with dreadlocks, the young white guys in Wu-Tang Clan t-shirts, the teenagers immersed in the swing lounge scene, the pot-bellied Italian men of a certain age smoking cigars. And sitting front row center, wearing a party colored muu-muu, Nick's next door neighbor Jean, who has lived at 124 East Fourth Street for the last 48 years. For all of them and for me, there is something about Frank Sinatra and something about how Nick Drakides interprets Frank Sinatra that bewitches us, that touches us.
<v Blake Eskin>他们当然会产生共鸣。梳着脏辫的黑人小伙、穿着武当帮T恤的年轻白人小伙、沉浸在摇摆乐休息室场景中的青少年、抽着雪茄的中年大腹便便的意大利男人也一样。坐在前排正中央、穿着五颜六色夏威夷长衫的是尼克的隔壁邻居琼，她在东第四街124号已经住了48年。对他们所有人以及对我来说，关于弗兰克·辛纳屈，以及关于尼克·德拉基德斯如何诠释弗兰克·辛纳屈，有一种魔力在迷住我们，在触动我们。

00:55:06.670 --> 00:55:32.590
<v Nick Drakides>There's a guy who lives next door. And he embraced me. He hugged me, this old Chinese guy, man, with a hearing aid. I'm like, I touched this guy. And I don't how I did it, but I did it.
<v Nick Drakides>有个邻居住在隔壁。他拥抱了我。他抱了我，这个戴着助听器的老中国老头，老兄。我想，我感动了这个家伙。我不知道我是怎么做到的，但我做到了。

00:55:32.590 --> 00:56:15.100
<v Blake Eskin>For any New Yorker to do something as big as this for his neighbors again and again is more than an anomaly. It is as rare and unstable as the elements at the bottom of the periodic table. The key ingredients of this event, neighborliness, generosity, free time, good weather, cooperative police officers, are hard to come by in this city. And they are nearly impossible to find together in the same place week after week. The Nick and Laraine show has had a longer run than anyone could expect. And something-- rain or the first frost or the 9th precinct or a Friday night gig with the Starlight Orchestra-- will soon bring it to a halt.
<v Blake Eskin>对于任何一个纽约人来说，为他的邻居一次又一次地做这样一件大事，已经不仅仅是一个反常现象了。它就像元素周期表底部的元素一样罕见且不稳定。这场活动的几个关键要素——邻里情谊、慷慨、闲暇时间、好天气、配合的警官——在这个城市里很难同时遇到。而且几乎不可能周复一周地在同一个地方同时找到它们。尼克和拉兰的表演比任何人预期的都要持久。总有什么东西——雨水、初霜、第九分局，或者是与星光乐队的周五晚演出——很快就会让它停下来。

00:56:15.100 --> 00:56:56.130
<v Blake Eskin>There's a gossip columnist in the New York Post named Cindy Adams. And it is tempting to resort to her mantra "only in New York folks, only in New York" to explain this phenomenon. But in Nick's case, the wisdom of Cindy Adams does not suffice. This is not the stuff of New York, not of the real New York or even of the New York of a bygone era, but of a mythical movie New York, a lower east side block built on a studio back lot. It is the first reel of an unknown MGM musical from just after the war, and it stars Nick Drakides. What happens in the rest of the film is anyone's guess.
<v Blake Eskin>《纽约邮报》里有一个名叫辛迪·亚当斯的八卦专栏作家。人们很容易借用她的口头禅“只有在纽约，各位，只有在纽约”来解释这种现象。但在尼克的情况下，辛迪·亚当斯的智慧是不够用的。这不是纽约的真实写照，不是真正的纽约，甚至不是逝去时代的纽约，而是一个神话般的电影中的纽约，一个建在摄影棚外景地上的下东区街区。这是一部战后不久的不知名米高梅歌舞片的第一盘胶片，而它的主演是尼克·德拉基德斯。电影剩下的部分会发生什么，谁也猜不准。

00:56:56.130 --> 00:56:59.940
<v Blake Eskin>[MUSIC - "STRANGERS IN THE NIGHT" BY FRANK SINATRA]
<v Blake Eskin>[音乐 - 弗兰克·辛纳屈的《夜半陌生人》]

00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:02.360
<v Ira Glass>Blake Eskin in New York.
<v Ira Glass>布莱克·埃斯金在纽约报道。

00:57:02.360 --> 00:57:16.830
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Nancy Updike and myself, with Alix Spiegel and Julie Snyder. Senior editor for this episode of our show is Paul Tough. Seth Lind is our production manager. Production help from Rachel Day, Aaron Scott, and WHYY in Philadelphia.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我们的节目今天由南希·厄普代克和我本人制作，阿利克斯·斯皮格尔和朱莉·斯奈德协助。本期节目的高级编辑是保罗·托夫。塞思·林德是我们的制作经理。制作协助来自雷切尔·戴、亚伦·斯科特以及费城的WHYY电台。

